arning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 29 Aug 2025 (this version), latest version 5 Jun 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Standard vs. Modular Sampling: Best Practices for Reliable LLM Unlearning\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Praveen Bushipaka , cite7†Lucia Passaro , cite8†Tommaso Cucinotta L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Standard vs. Modular Sampling: Best Practices for Reliable LLM Unlearning, by Praveen Bushipaka and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:A conventional LLM Unlearning setting consists of two subsets -\"forget\" and \"retain\", with the objectives of removing the undesired knowledge from the forget set while preserving the remaining knowledge from the retain. In privacy-focused unlearning research, a retain set is often further divided into neighbor sets, containing either directly or indirectly connected to the forget targets; and augmented by a general-knowledge set.\nL17: A common practice in existing benchmarks is to employ only a single neighbor set, with general knowledge which fails to reflect the real-world data complexities and relationships. LLM Unlearning typically involves 1:1 sampling or cyclic iteration sampling. However, the efficacy and stability of these de facto standards have not been critically examined. In this study, we systematically evaluate these common practices.\nL18: Our findings reveal that relying on a single neighbor set is suboptimal and that a standard sampling approach can obscure performance trade-offs. Based on this analysis, we propose and validate an initial set of best practices: (1) Incorporation of diverse neighbor sets to balance forget efficacy and model utility, (2) Standard 1:1 sampling methods are inefficient and yield poor results, (3) Our proposed Modular Entity-Level Unlearning (MELU) strategy as an alternative to cyclic sampling.\nL19: We demonstrate that this modular approach, combined with robust algorithms, provides a clear and stable path towards effective unlearning.\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2509.05316 [cs.LG]\nL22:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2509.05316v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL23:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.05316†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Praveen Bushipaka [cite14†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Fri, 29 Aug 2025 19:25:52 UTC (6,224 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Fri, 5 Jun 2026 13:49:14 UTC (6,224 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Standard vs. Modular Sampling: Best Practices for Reliable LLM Unlearning, by Praveen Bushipaka and 1 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite9†View PDF L37:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite15†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite16†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.LG\nL44: \nL45: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L46: \nL47: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2025-09 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite22†cs L52: cite23†cs.AI L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite27†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Connected Papers (cite28†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL84: \nL85: Litmaps (cit